# Publish the bundled default configuration and layout into the build
# variant's `config/` directory.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# `ct` reads these two files from `<prefix>/config/` at runtime -- `configDir`
# in `src/common/config.nim`, where `<prefix>` is `codetracerPrefix`, the
# directory above `bin/ct`, i.e. the variant root.  `loadConfig` copies
# `configDir/default_config.yaml` into the user's config directory the first
# time CodeTracer runs on a machine, so on a pristine profile a missing file
# there is not a degraded mode -- it is an uncaught `OSError` before anything
# else happens:
#
#     Error: Unhandled exception
#       src/ct/cli/build.nim build
#       src/common/config.nim(159) loadConfig
#       .../osfiles.nim(241) copyFile
#     Unhandled No such file or directory
#     Additional info: .../src/build-debug/config/default_config.yaml
#
# Until this rule existed, publishing them was nobody's job.  `../Tupfile`
# asserted that they "already reach build-debug/config/ via the variant source
# mirror"; they do not.  Tup's variant mirror reproduces the source DIRECTORY
# tree into the variant and copies no files -- which is why `build-debug/lsp`,
# `build-debug/tools` and `build-debug/bpf-monitor` all exist and are all
# empty, and why `build-debug/config` existed as an empty directory that looked
# right in an `ls` of the variant root.  Tup's own database agrees: before this
# rule, `default_config.yaml` and `default_layout.json` appeared in it only as
# type-0 (source) nodes under `src/config`, with no generated node anywhere.
#
# Everyone downstream had already worked around it by hand:
#   * non-nix-build/windows/setup-codetracer-runtime-env.{ps1,sh} copy the two
#     files into `<build-debug>/config/` themselves, with a comment saying the
#     runtime expects them there;
#   * scripts/docs/deep-review-capture-lib.sh refuses to start and tells you to
#     `mkdir -p .../config && cp ...` yourself;
#   * codetracer-specs/Planned-Features/Native-Target-Recognition.milestones.org
#     records an agent having to do the same `cp` to get a suite to execute.
# `repro.nim` does declare them (targets `config-default-layout-json` and
# `config-default-config-yaml`), but reprobuild writes to
# `src/build-<config>-repro`, not to tup's `src/build-debug`, so on the tup
# branch -- which is what `just build-once` takes on Linux by default -- that
# declaration never applied.
#
# WHY A TUPFILE OF ITS OWN, RATHER THAN A RULE IN ../Tupfile
# ----------------------------------------------------------
# `!tup_preserve` from the parent directory would need input and output
# relative paths that differ (see the note in `../Tupfile`); a rule whose
# Tupfile sits in the same directory as its inputs has no such clash.  This is
# the same shape as `../public/resources/Tupfile` and `../public/third_party/
# Tupfile`.
#
# `*.*` rather than an explicit two-file list on purpose: the failure mode
# being fixed here is a runtime asset that silently is not published, so a new
# file added to `src/config/` should be published by default rather than
# forgotten.  (`scripts/require-tup-globs.sh` deliberately does not check a
# bare `*.*` -- it globs the Tupfile's own directory, which exists by
# construction.)
#
# THE GUARD
# ---------
# `scripts/require-runtime-assets.sh` runs at the end of
# `scripts/build-once.sh` on BOTH build branches and fails by name if the
# startup asset closure is ever incomplete again.  It derives the file names
# from `src/common/config.nim` rather than repeating them, so a rename cannot
# leave the guard checking the old names.
: foreach *.* |> !tup_preserve |>
